  34:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c **** 
  35:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c **** /**
  36:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * @defgroup Corr Correlation
  37:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  38:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Correlation is a mathematical operation that is similar to convolution.
  39:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * As with convolution, correlation uses two signals to produce a third signal.
  40:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The underlying algorithms in correlation and convolution are identical except that one of the in
  41:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Correlation is commonly used to measure the similarity between two signals.
  42:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * It has applications in pattern recognition, cryptanalysis, and searching.
  43:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The CMSIS library provides correlation functions for Q7, Q15, Q31 and floating-point data types.
  44:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Fast versions of the Q15 and Q31 functions are also provided.
  45:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  46:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par Algorithm
  47:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Let <code>a[n]</code> and <code>b[n]</code> be sequences of length <code>srcALen</code> and <cod
  48:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The convolution of the two signals is denoted by
  49:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  50:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *                   c[n] = a[n] * b[n]
  51:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * </pre>
  52:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * In correlation, one of the signals is flipped in time
  53:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * <pre>
  54:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *                   c[n] = a[n] * b[-n]
  55:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * </pre>
  56:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  57:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par
  58:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * and this is mathematically defined as
  59:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \image html CorrelateEquation.gif
  60:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par
  61:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The <code>pSrcA</code> points to the first input vector of length <code>srcALen</code> and <code
  62:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The result <code>c[n]</code> is of length <code>2 * max(srcALen, srcBLen) - 1</code> and is defi
  63:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The output result is written to <code>pDst</code> and the calling function must allocate <code>2
  64:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  65:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * <b>Note</b>
  66:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par
  67:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * The <code>pDst</code> should be initialized to all zeros before being used.
  68:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  69:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * <b>Fixed-Point Behavior</b>
  70:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par
  71:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Correlation requires summing up a large number of intermediate products.
  72:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * As such, the Q7, Q15, and Q31 functions run a risk of overflow and saturation.
  73:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Refer to the function specific documentation below for further details of the particular algorit
  74:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  75:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  76:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * <b>Fast Versions</b>
  77:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  78:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par
  79:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Fast versions are supported for Q31 and Q15.  Cycles for Fast versions are less compared to Q31 
  80:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * the input signals should be scaled down to avoid intermediate overflows.
  81:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  82:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  83:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * <b>Opt Versions</b>
  84:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  *
  85:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * \par
  86:Drivers/CMSIS/DSP/Source/FilteringFunctions/arm_correlate_f32.c ****  * Opt versions are supported for Q15 and Q7.  Design uses internal scratch buffer for getting good
